@baseUrl = http://localhost:8083

# ============================================================================
#  Seven Database Laws — sequential endpoint tester (VS Code REST Client)
# ============================================================================
#  Prerequisite: PostgreSQL must be up, then run the app on 8083:
#      make start        (suite: Postgres :5432, api-rules :8080, db-laws :8083)
#  NOTE: the db-laws app listens on 8083 (8080 is the sibling api-rules app,
#  which requires an API-Version header and would otherwise answer 400).
#
#  Run the requests in order, top to bottom. Comments mark expected responses,
#  and several requests reference the response of an earlier one (e.g. the
#  product created in the transaction lesson). If you only execute a snippet,
#  run its dependencies first.

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 1 — Start with the queries
#  The schema exists because of the screens that read it. Every endpoint below
#  is backed by an index whose name (idx_*) is the same as why it was created.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 1a — Catalogue page: filter by category (served by idx_products_category)
###
### > Expected: 200 — totalElements 1, all content[].category == "grocery"
GET {{baseUrl}}/products?category=grocery

### Lesson 1b — Catalogue page: sort + paginate (served by idx_products_name)
GET {{baseUrl}}/products?sort=price:desc&page=0&size=3

### Lesson 1c — Customer order history (served by idx_orders_customer_id + order_items FK)
###
### > Expected: 200 — array of orders for customer 1, newest first
GET {{baseUrl}}/customers/1/orders

### Lesson 1d — Finance dashboard: sales grouped by month (served by idx_orders_created_at)
###
### > Expected: 200 — [ { "year":…, "month":…, "total":… } ] — GROUP BY ran in the DB
GET {{baseUrl}}/stats/monthly-sales

### Lesson 1e — Top customers by spend (aggregate access pattern)
GET {{baseUrl}}/stats/top-customers

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 2 — Ask what happens if the data is wrong
#  POST /orders is ONE transaction: stock decrements + order rows commit
#  together or roll back together. A failing later line undoes earlier lines.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 2a — Create a fresh low-stock product for the demo
# @name scarceProduct
POST {{baseUrl}}/products
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rare Tool",
  "category": "tools",
  "price": 99.00,
  "currency": "USD",
  "stockQuantity": 1
}

### Lesson 2b — Buy it: stock drops atomically with the order row
###
### > Expected: 201 — totalAmount = unitPrice × quantity, status "CREATED"
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Demo Buyer",
  "customerEmail": "buyer@example.com",
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{scarceProduct.response.body.$.id}}, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}

### Lesson 2c — Now try to oversell the last unit: whole transaction rolls back
###
### > Expected: 409 — code "INSUFFICIENT_STOCK". The product still has 1 unit.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Greedy Buyer",
  "customerEmail": "greedy@example.com",
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{scarceProduct.response.body.$.id}}, "quantity": 2 }
  ]
}

### Lesson 2d — Proof: the guarded UPDATE rejected the over-sale (still 1)
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/{{scarceProduct.response.body.$.id}}

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 3 — Don't replace the database before finding the bottleneck
#  The fixes are queries and indexes, not a new database. Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE
#  (see exercises.md Lesson 3) and watch the plan use idx_products_category.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 3a — The "slow" catalogue query is an Index Scan in disguise
GET {{baseUrl}}/products?category=tools

### Lesson 3b — Missing filter value: still a cheap parameterised index query
GET {{baseUrl}}/products

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 4 — Understand how it scales
#  Read-heavy app: order history + catalogue consume the reads. The eager
#  fetch below is a scaling win with no new database (kills the N+1).
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 4a — Order detail: items fetched in ONE query via @EntityGraph
###
### > Expected: 200 — order with its items[] array populated in a single query
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/1

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 5 — Know when one database is enough
#  Everything (catalogue, orders, reports) is one Postgres, and the tests run
#  the same code on H2-in-memory with zero changes — see application-test.yml.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 5a — One database answers the catalogue...
GET {{baseUrl}}/products

### Lesson 5b — ...and the reports, with no second store added
GET {{baseUrl}}/stats/top-customers

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 6 — Don't confuse fast with durable
#  The app is fast (indexed reads) AND durable (Postgres WAL + fsync). H2 in
#  tests is fast but NOT durable — the "fast vs durable" trade, on purpose.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 6a — A committed order survives a restart (Postgres WAL), smoke-run
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Durability Check",
  "customerEmail": "durable@example.com",
  "items": [
    { "productId": 3, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 7 — Think about what happens after deployment
#  docker-compose.yml gives healthchecks + a volume; schema-indexes.sql is the
#  manual Flyway-style migration. Observe: curl the live container health.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 7a — The DB reports healthy (broker/watchdogs rely on this)
GET {{baseUrl}}/customers

### Lesson 7b — Consistent error shape when the database says "not found"
###
### > Expected: 404 — { "status":404, "code":"PRODUCT_NOT_FOUND", ... }
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/99999